srow(119, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND WHEN YOU SEND HIM FREE FROM YOU … FURNISH, YOU SHALL FURNISH HIM" (15:13-14) — MIGHT ONE FURNISH ONLY THE ONE WHO GOES OUT AT SIX YEARS? WHENCE THE ONE WHO GOES OUT AT THE JUBILEE, AT THE MASTER\'S DEATH, AND THE HEBREW MAIDSERVANT BY THE SIGNS? "SEND", "WHEN YOU SEND", "AND WHEN YOU SEND HIM". MIGHT YOU FURNISH EVEN THE ONE SENT OUT BY MONEY? "AND WHEN YOU SEND HIM FREE FROM YOU" — THE ONE YOU SEND YOU FURNISH, NOT THE ONE WHOSE SENDING IS HIS OWN. The head row of piska 119 ({SP_(119, 1, "אין", "מעניקים", "אלא", "ליוצא", "בשש")} "furnish only the one who goes out at six"; {SP_(119, 1, "למי", "שאתה", "משלחו", "אתה", "מעניק")} "the one you send you furnish"): THE GIFT\'S CASES FROM THE VERB SAID THREE TIMES — the jubilee, the master\'s death, the maidservant\'s signs in; the self-redeemer out; the compile: the gift an effect of the master\'s sending, not of the slave\'s exit. Fresh.')
srow(119, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'WHENCE THAT IF YOU FURNISHED HIM ONCE, YOU FURNISH HIM EVEN A HUNDRED TIMES? THE TEACHING SAYS "FURNISH, YOU SHALL FURNISH". ({SP_(119, 2, "אפלו", "מאה", "פעמים")} "even a hundred times"): THE FOURTH DOUBLING READ AS REPETITION (116:12, 117:6 the form) — "furnish" the verb\'s ONE seat in the Torah (asserted: 15:14 has no kin by computation); the English\'s row carries the money case of 119:1 as well (the two files\' division differs — both read whole). Fresh.')
srow(119, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"HIM" — AND NOT HIS HEIRS. ({SP_(119, 3, "ולא", "ליורשיו")} "and not his heirs"): THE GIFT PERSONAL — the pronoun\'s limit (118:6\'s heir on the other side: the master\'s heir no master, the slave\'s heir no taker). Fresh.')
srow(119, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'"FROM YOUR FLOCK, YOUR FLOOR AND YOUR PRESS" — MIGHT ONE FURNISH ONLY FROM THE FLOCK, THE FLOOR AND THE PRESS NAMED? WHENCE TO INCLUDE EVERYTHING? "FURNISH, YOU SHALL FURNISH" — TO INCLUDE EVERYTHING. THEN WHY "FROM YOUR FLOCK, YOUR FLOOR AND YOUR PRESS"? AS THE FLOCK, THE FLOOR AND THE PRESS ARE PARTICULAR IN BEING FIT FOR A BLESSING — MONEY IS OUT, WHICH IS NOT FIT FOR A BLESSING: R. SHIMON\'S WORDS. R. ELIEZER BEN JACOB SAYS: MULES ARE OUT, WHICH DO NOT BEAR. ({SP_(119, 4, "לרבות", "כל", "דבר")} "to include everything"; {SP_(119, 4, "יצאו", "כספים")} "money is out"; {SP_(119, 4, "יצאו", "פרדות")} "mules are out"): I6 (checked) BY SHAPE — the general "furnish", the particular list, the general "which He blessed you": ONLY WHAT RESEMBLES THE PARTICULAR, and the resembling feature a DISPUTE — fit for a benediction (R. Shimon, money out) or bearing young (R. Eliezer ben Jacob, mules out); the exam reads the verse in that form (Kiddushin 17a); the English\'s note on the benedictions of enjoyment (Mishnah Berakhot 6). Fresh.')
srow(119, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'"WITH WHICH THE LORD YOUR GOD HAS BLESSED YOU" — MIGHT IT BE THAT IF THE HOUSE WAS BLESSED BECAUSE OF HIM THEY FURNISH HIM, AND IF NOT, NOT? "FURNISH, YOU SHALL FURNISH HIM" — IN ANY CASE. THEN WHY "WITH WHICH THE LORD YOUR GOD HAS BLESSED YOU, YOU SHALL GIVE HIM"? ALL ACCORDING TO THE BLESSING YOU FURNISH HIM. ({SP_(119, 5, "נתברך", "בית", "בגללו")} "the house was blessed because of him"; {SP_(119, 5, "הכל", "לפי", "הברכה")} "all according to the blessing"): THE GIFT UNCONDITIONAL, ITS MEASURE THE BLESSING — the clause a scale, not a condition (117:8\'s form at the other verse); the compile: a floor and a measure. Fresh.')
srow(120, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL REMEMBER THAT YOU WERE A SLAVE IN THE LAND OF EGYPT" (15:15) — AS IN EGYPT I FURNISHED YOU AND FURNISHED YOU AGAIN, SO YOU FURNISH HIM AND FURNISH HIM AGAIN; AS IN EGYPT I GAVE YOU WITH AN OPEN HAND, SO YOU GIVE HIM WITH AN OPEN HAND. AND SO IT SAYS "THE WINGS OF THE DOVE COVERED WITH SILVER" (PSALM 68:14) — THE SPOIL OF EGYPT, "AND HER PINIONS WITH YELLOW GOLD" — THE SPOIL OF THE SEA; "WE WILL MAKE YOU CIRCLETS OF GOLD" (SONG 1:11) — THE SPOIL OF THE SEA, "WITH STUDS OF SILVER" — THE SPOIL OF EGYPT. The head row of piska 120 ({SP_(120, 1, "הענקתי", "לך", "ושניתי", "לך")} "I furnished you and furnished you again"; {SP_(120, 1, "ברחוב", "יד")} "with an open hand"; {SP_(120, 1, "זו", "בזת", "מצרים")} "this is the spoil of Egypt"): THE MEMORY CLAUSE READ AS THE MODEL OF THE GIFT — the exodus\'s spoil (Exodus 3:21 "you shall not go out empty" and 12:36 the kin, asserted: "empty" at 15:13 the same word) the measure of the slave\'s gift; the two verses of silver and gold the two spoils — the English\'s note the Mekhilta\'s twin (pischa 13); the chapter\'s one narrative verb "redeemed you" (asserted) inside this clause. Fresh.')
srow(120, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'"THEREFORE I COMMAND YOU THIS THING TODAY" — BY DAY THEY PIERCE, AND THEY DO NOT PIERCE AT NIGHT. ({SP_(120, 2, "ביום", "רוצעים")} "by day they pierce"; {SP_(120, 2, "ואין", "רוצעים", "בלילה")} "and they do not pierce at night"): "TODAY" READ AS A TIME DATUM FOR THE NEXT VERSES\' RITE — the piercing a daytime act (the compile: a clock condition on the awl); the fifth "therefore I command you" of the book (asserted). Fresh.')
srow(121, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND IT SHALL BE, IF HE SAY TO YOU: I WILL NOT GO OUT FROM YOU" (15:16) — MIGHT IT BE ONCE? THE TEACHING SAYS "AND IF THE SLAVE SAY, SAY" (EXODUS 21:5) — UNTIL HE SAYS AND REPEATS. The head row of piska 121 ({SP_(121, 1, "עד", "שיאמר", "וישנה")} "until he says and repeats"): THE TWIN LAW\'S DOUBLING READ AS TWO SAYINGS — the slave\'s declaration a repeated act (the English\'s note Mekhilta neziqin 2); the first person "I will not go out" the slave\'s one word in the chapter (asserted). Fresh.')
srow(121, 2, 'MATERIAL', f'IF HE SAID IT WITHIN THE SIX AND NOT AT THE END OF THE SIX — HE IS NOT PIERCED, AS IT IS SAID "I WILL NOT GO OUT FREE" (EXODUS 21:5) — UNTIL HE SAYS IT AT THE HOUR OF GOING OUT. IF HE SAID IT AT THE END OF THE SIX AND NOT WITHIN THE SIX — HE IS NOT PIERCED, AS IT IS SAID "AND IF THE SLAVE SAY, SAY" — UNTIL HE SAYS IT WHILE HE IS A SLAVE. ({SP_(121, 2, "בתוך", "שש")} "within the six"; {SP_(121, 2, "בשעת", "יציאה")} "at the hour of going out"; {SP_(121, 2, "בשעה", "שהוא", "עבד")} "while he is a slave"): THE TWO SAYINGS TIMED — one inside the term, one at its edge, both from the twin\'s two clauses (the English cites the second as Deuteronomy 21:5, its slip); the compile: two timestamps on the declaration. Fresh.')
srow(121, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"BECAUSE HE LOVES YOU" — SINCE IT IS SAID "I LOVE MY MASTER" (EXODUS 21:5), FROM HERE YOU SAY: HE LOVED HIS MASTER AND HIS MASTER DID NOT LOVE HIM, OR HE WAS LOVED BY HIS MASTER AND HE DID NOT LOVE HIS MASTER — HE IS NOT PIERCED, AS IT IS SAID "BECAUSE HE LOVES YOU". HE HAS A WIFE AND CHILDREN AND HIS MASTER HAS NOT — HE IS NOT PIERCED, AS IT IS SAID "BECAUSE HE LOVES YOU AND YOUR HOUSE". ({SP_(121, 3, "היה", "הוא", "אוהב", "את", "רבו")} "he loved his master"; {SP_(121, 3, "ורבו", "אין", "אוהבו")} "and his master did not love him"): THE TWO VERSES\' TWO LOVES READ AS ONE MUTUAL CONDITION — Exodus 21:5\'s "I love my master, my wife and my children" and this verse\'s "he loves you and your house" (asserted: the twin diffed — the slave\'s family there, the master\'s house here); "your house" the master\'s family (Onkelos "the people of your house"); the bracketed clause one editor\'s (the English\'s note). Fresh.')
srow(121, 4, 'MATERIAL', f'"BECAUSE IT IS WELL WITH HIM WITH YOU" — SO IF HE FELL ILL, OR HIS MASTER, HE IS NOT PIERCED. ({SP_(121, 4, "אם", "חלה", "הוא", "או", "רבו")} "if he fell ill, or his master"): THE WELL-BEING A CONDITION ON BOTH — illness in either bars the rite (118:7\'s illness inside the term the other seat); 23:17\'s escaped slave "where it is good for him" the kin (asserted). Fresh.')
srow(122, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL TAKE THE AWL" (15:17) — WHENCE TO INCLUDE THE THORN, THE GLASS AND THE REED-SLIVER? "AND YOU SHALL TAKE": R. YOSE SON OF R. JUDAH\'S WORDS. RABBI SAYS: "THE AWL" — AS THE AWL IS PARTICULAR IN BEING OF METAL, I HAVE ONLY METAL. FROM HERE R. ISHMAEL WOULD SAY: IN THREE PLACES THE HALAKHA CIRCUMVENTS SCRIPTURE — THE TORAH SAID "AND COVER IT WITH DUST" (LEVITICUS 17:13) AND THE HALAKHA SAID: WITH ANYTHING THAT GROWS PLANTS; THE TORAH SAID "AND WRITE HER A BILL OF DIVORCE" (24:1) AND THE HALAKHA SAID: ON ANYTHING DETACHED; THE TORAH SAID "WITH THE AWL" AND THE HALAKHA SAID: WITH ANYTHING. RABBI SAYS: "AT THE DOOR OR THE DOORPOST" (EXODUS 21:6) — STANDING. The head row of piska 122 ({SP_(122, 1, "בשלשה", "מקומות", "הלכה", "עוקפת", "המקרא")} "in three places the halakha circumvents Scripture"; {SP_(122, 1, "והלכה", "אמרה", "בכל", "דבר")} "and the halakha said: with anything"; {SP_(122, 1, "מעמד")} "standing"): THE ORAL LAW OVERRIDING THE WRITTEN PARTICULAR, NAMED AS A RULE OF THREE — the blood\'s dust, the divorce\'s writ, the awl: the code/data law\'s hardest case inside the spine (R. Ishmael\'s list, the English\'s note: the Mekhilta\'s versions perhaps a scribe\'s addition); the tool a DISPUTE (any tool by "take", metal by "the awl"); the posture from Exodus 21:6\'s doorpost, absent here (asserted: no doorpost, no judges in 15:17) — NEVER READ AHEAD: 24:1 waits for its sitting; the exam: Kiddushin 21b. Fresh.')
srow(122, 2, 'CONTEXT', f'"AWL" — THIS IS A LARGE AWL. The row without a citation ({SP_(122, 2, "זה", "מרצע", "גדול")} "this is a large awl"): the tool\'s size (the exam: Kiddushin 21b). Fresh.')
srow(122, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND YOU SHALL TAKE THE AWL" — I MIGHT HEAR: BETWEEN HIM AND HIMSELF? THE TEACHING SAYS "AND HIS MASTER SHALL BRING HIM TO THE JUDGES" (EXODUS 21:6) — TO THE JUDGES, THAT IT BE DONE THROUGH THOSE WHO SOLD HIM. ({SP_(122, 3, "בינו", "לבין", "עצמו")} "between him and himself"; {SP_(122, 3, "אצל", "הדינים")} "to the judges"): THE RITE NOT PRIVATE — the court present, from the twin\'s clause this verse omits (asserted: no "judges" in 15:17; Onkelos at Exodus 21:6 the kin\'s reading, no Onkelos row of it in any ledger); the English\'s "present him to traders" its own gloss. Fresh.')
srow(122, 4, 'CONTEXT', f'"WITH THE AWL" (EXODUS 21:6) — WITH ANYTHING THAT MAKES A MARK. ({SP_(122, 4, "בכל", "דבר", "שהוא", "רושם")} "with anything that makes a mark"): 122:1\'s first arm restated. Fresh.')
srow(122, 5, 'MATERIAL', f'R. ELAZAR SAID: DID NOT YUDAN BERABBI EXPOUND THAT THEY PIERCE ONLY THE LOBE? AND THE SAGES SAY: A PRIEST IS NOT PIERCED, LEST HE BECOME BLEMISHED — IF HE IS PIERCED IN THE LOBE, HOW DOES HE BECOME BLEMISHED? RATHER IT TEACHES THAT THEY PIERCE ONLY THE UPPER PART OF THE EAR. R. MEIR SAYS: EVEN THE CARTILAGE; FOR R. MEIR WOULD SAY: A PRIEST IS NOT PIERCED. The row without a citation ({SP_(122, 5, "אין", "כהן", "נרצע")} "a priest is not pierced"; {SP_(122, 5, "בגבוה", "שבאזן")} "in the upper part of the ear"): THE PLACE ON THE EAR DECIDED BY THE PRIEST\'S BLEMISH — a rule found from another rule\'s consequence (the priest barred from the altar by a bored ear, Leviticus 21:17-23 the kin\'s ledger); a DISPUTE on the cartilage; the English\'s "our colleague" for beRabbi. Fresh.')
srow(122, 6, 'MATERIAL', f'"HIS EAR" — "HIS EAR" IS SAID HERE AND "HIS EAR" IS SAID THERE (LEVITICUS 14:14): AS THE EAR SAID THERE IS THE RIGHT ONE, SO THE EAR SAID HERE IS THE RIGHT ONE, IN THE UPPER PART OF THE EAR. ({SP_(122, 6, "נאמר", "כאן", "אזנו", "ונאמר", "להלן")} "his ear is said here and his ear is said there"; {SP_(122, 6, "ימנית")} "the right one"): I2 (checked) WITH THE LEPER\'S RITE — the right ear from Leviticus 14:14\'s "the lobe of the right ear" (Leviticus 14 read at its sitting); the analogy on a body part, the third I2 of the chapter (111:7, 117:3). Fresh.')
srow(122, 7, 'MATERIAL', f'"IN HIS EAR AND IN THE DOOR" — MIGHT IT BE AT THE SIDE OF HIS EAR? THE TEACHING SAYS "IN HIS EAR AND IN THE DOOR" — IT TELLS THAT HE PUTS IT IN THE EAR UNTIL IT REACHES THE DOOR. ({SP_(122, 7, "עד", "שמגיע", "לדלת")} "until it reaches the door"): THE TWO OBJECTS ONE STROKE — through the ear into the door (the verse\'s "in the door" for Exodus 21:6\'s "to the door or the doorpost", asserted: one token shared in order). Fresh.')
srow(122, 8, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND HE SHALL BE YOUR SERVANT FOR EVER" — ALL THE DAYS OF THE MASTER\'S WORLD, EVEN IF SOLD THIRTY OR FORTY YEARS BEFORE THE JUBILEE. FROM HERE YOU SAY: A HEBREW SLAVE SERVES THE SON AND NOT THE DAUGHTER; THE PIERCED ONE, NEITHER THE SON NOR THE DAUGHTER. WHENCE TO GIVE WHAT IS SAID HERE TO THERE AND WHAT IS SAID THERE TO HERE? "FOR EVER", "FOR EVER" (EXODUS 21:6) FOR AN ANALOGY. ({SP_(122, 8, "כל", "ימי", "עולמו", "של", "אדון")} "all the days of the master\'s world"; {SP_(122, 8, "עולם", "עולם", "לגזרה", "שוה")} "for ever, for ever, for an analogy"): "FOR EVER" READ AS THE MASTER\'S LIFETIME (the jubilee inside it — Leviticus 25:40\'s exit at the jubilee the kin, read at its sitting; the exam: Kiddushin 15a, 21b), THE SON NOT THE DAUGHTER (118:6\'s son), THE PIERCED SERVES NO HEIR — and I2 (checked) NAMED IN THE HEBREW on the word "for ever" at 15:17 and Exodus 21:6 (asserted: "servant for ever" ONE seat in this form), the fourth analogy of the chapter and the second named by the shelf itself (111:7). Fresh.')
srow(122, 9, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND ALSO TO YOUR MAIDSERVANT YOU SHALL DO LIKEWISE" — TO FURNISH. MIGHT IT BE EVEN FOR THE PIERCING? THE TEACHING SAYS "AND IF THE SLAVE SAY, SAY" (EXODUS 21:5) — THE SLAVE, AND NOT THE MAIDSERVANT. ({SP_(122, 9, "להעניק")} "to furnish"; {SP_(122, 9, "העבד", "ולא", "האמה")} "the slave, and not the maidservant"): THE DESIGN\'S QUESTION ANSWERED — "LIKEWISE" REACHES THE GIFT, NOT THE AWL: the maidservant furnished at her exit, never pierced (Exodus 21:7\'s "she shall not go out as the menservants do" the twin the shelf reconciles by "the slave"); the compile: the gift effect on both sexes, the piercing on one. Fresh.')
srow(123, 1, 'MATERIAL', f'"LET IT NOT BE HARD IN YOUR EYES WHEN YOU SEND HIM" (15:18) — FROM HERE YOU SAY: A HIRELING WORKS BY DAY; THIS ONE, BY DAY AND BY NIGHT. The head row of piska 123 ({SP_(123, 1, "שכיר", "עובד", "ביום")} "a hireling works by day"; {SP_(123, 1, "ביום", "ובלילה")} "by day and by night"): "DOUBLE THE HIRE OF A HIRELING" READ AS THE NIGHT\'S SERVICE — the slave\'s worth twice the hireling\'s because his night is the master\'s (the English\'s note: THE MEKHILTA SAYS THE OPPOSITE — the slave too works only by day; a DISPUTE of two spines); the exam: Kiddushin 15a (the double read as the maidservant\'s designation — a third reading); "double" the Torah\'s seven, 17:18\'s copy of the law the same word (asserted). Fresh.')
srow(123, 2, 'CONTEXT', f'"AND THE LORD YOUR GOD WILL BLESS YOU" — MIGHT IT BE WHEN IDLE? THE TEACHING SAYS "IN ALL THAT YOU DO". ({SP_(123, 2, "יכול", "בטל")} "might it be when idle"): the blessing on the work, not the idleness (14:29\'s "in all the work of your hand" the twin form). Fresh.')
srow(123, 3, 'MATERIAL', f'"AND THE LORD YOUR GOD WILL BLESS YOU" — WHEREVER SCRIPTURE PENALIZES MONEY, A BLESSING IS WRITTEN, AND MOST OF ALL WHERE THERE IS A LOSS OF PURSE. ({SP_(123, 3, "עונש", "ממון")} "penalizes money"; {SP_(123, 3, "חסרון", "כיס")} "a loss of purse"): A RULE OF THE BOOK\'S FORM — the money-law\'s blessing clause a fixed pair (the chapter\'s own: 15:4, 15:6, 15:10, 15:14, 15:18 — the blessing verb\'s six tokens here, asserted); the English\'s note Sifra Tzav 1:1. Fresh.')
